WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:12.000
Welcome to the lecture on database implementation. Today we want to look at the first big part of database architecture, namely storage.

2
00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:16.840
So the problem that we want to address here is conceptually very simple.

3
00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:26.340
So on the upper end here we have application data, we have some kind of user data that is semantically stored in the database.

4
00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.920
And as a lower level, we have how the data is really stored.

5
00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.180
Like at the lowest level, of course, we have the physical drive.

6
00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:35.660
This is something where the data is physically written to.

7
00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:40.460
Then we might have a logical drive on top of this, for example, because of a rate array

8
00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:41.420
or volume manager.

9
00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:47.280
So this here, from our perspective, usually is the drive, if you wish.

10
00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:52.080
So there's a drive here, logical, physical drive.

11
00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:53.520
Then we have a file system.

12
00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.920
that is provided to us by the operating system,

13
00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:00.300
and then there must be some magical component in between,

14
00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:03.040
that somehow maps the application data

15
00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.580
down to the file system.

16
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:07.300
So the question is now, how do we do this?

17
00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:09.680
And it's not so obvious how to do this,

18
00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:11.700
because there are design choices that we can do.

19
00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.100
For example, the first question is,

20
00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:14.960
should we use the file system at all?

21
00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:18.560
Because we could say, we want to forget the file system,

22
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:22.100
instead we want to directly access the disk drive.

23
00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.460
Historically, this is what database systems have done.

24
00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.600
As well, they have preferred not to use a file system,

25
00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.140
but instead directly access the drive,

26
00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:33.020
because this gives you more control.

27
00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:35.780
So you have more fine-grained control,

28
00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:37.600
how data is stored.

29
00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:40.960
But in the long run, people stopped doing this

30
00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.260
because for administrator, it's a pain

31
00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:46.300
if the database system uses raw disk access

32
00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:47.340
instead of file systems,

33
00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:49.440
backups much more difficult and so on.

34
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.820
So people went away from doing this over the time.

35
00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.920
So we can, we have degrees of freedom

36
00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.220
in how we map application data to the drive

37
00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.540
and we want to look at different aspects of this.

38
00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:04.200
So this is what this part is all about.

39
00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:08.240
Now when talking about storage,

40
00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:11.360
we have of course a number of different requirements

41
00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:12.760
that we want to satisfy.

42
00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:17.560
First one is we want data independence.

43
00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.060
Now what does this mean data independence?

44
00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:26.640
So we want to decouple the data and the physical storage.

45
00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:28.180
Now why do we want to do this?

46
00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:35.980
First, what is absolutely important here is, we do not want that the application knows

47
00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:37.700
how the data is physically stored.

48
00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:44.140
So basically my application here, we write it once and then I store it to my disk.

49
00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:49.900
And then later I can change my disk, for example I can buy new disks or I can for example

50
00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:56.260
migrate from a rotating disk to SSDs or can introduce a rate array or might even

51
00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:01.340
introduce network components. Its application should be totally unaffected

52
00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:06.000
by this. The application logic has to be decoupled from physical storage. This is a

53
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:11.000
very good property because this gives us a freedom to reorganize my

54
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:17.600
hardware as it makes sense if data grows or excess patterns change without doing

55
00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:24.080
anything with the application. So data independence means we shield the user or I should say the

56
00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:28.540
application of the real user because real users usually don't even allow to access the database

57
00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:33.560
but we shield the application from physical storage. This we call data independence.

58
00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:43.000
Next point here is scalability. We want of course that the database system scales to nearly

59
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:46.520
arbitrary data sizes. Of course we can only say nearly because at some point

60
00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:51.460
we will not be able to scale, but if for example you can address 2 to the power of 64

61
00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:58.800
bytes, this is a reasonably large number. So this is an okay size. So this is usually what

62
00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:08.380
we consider large enough. So we want to scale to very large sizes and still even though we do this,

63
00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:10.060
we want, of course, fast access.

64
00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:16.000
So this means we must have a way, for example,

65
00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:19.980
to jump to an arbitrary data element in my data.

66
00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:25.900
So implicitly, this means we need some mechanism to say,

67
00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:28.220
okay, please retrieve this individual value.

68
00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:30.740
So we take one individual data item,

69
00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:32.840
and we want to be able to retrieve this

70
00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:34.660
without reading everything else.

71
00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:38.720
because if you would know that you only need

72
00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:40.220
sequential access to your data,

73
00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:42.580
you could make different implementation choices

74
00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:45.380
than if you need to be able to jump

75
00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:46.560
in the middle of the data.

76
00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:49.180
But we assume that you must jump

77
00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:50.060
into the middle of the data,

78
00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:52.760
which is clear why you need this.

79
00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:54.560
Because if somebody says something like this,

80
00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:57.200
if you say, I've retrieved something from my database,

81
00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.040
select star from R,

82
00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:12.060
where key is equal to 1, 2, 3,

83
00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:14.420
then of course we expect that this query here is fast.

84
00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:17.100
So we expect that we get an answer to this

85
00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:20.960
in a few milliseconds at most,

86
00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:22.560
hopefully faster.

87
00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:26.320
And this means we need to definitely leave random access

88
00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:28.220
to data as we use the boxes.

89
00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:32.620
Of course, we also want to be able to update arbitrary data.

90
00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:37.820
Again, this rules out some storage alternatives.

91
00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:39.300
For example, what you could do is,

92
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:41.820
we don't do this, but what you could do is

93
00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:42.980
you could take all your data,

94
00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:46.120
compress it very tightly into a nice condensed format.

95
00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:48.960
This has, of course, also advantages if you do this.

96
00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:52.600
But in general, we don't want to do this.

97
00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:54.540
At least not everything in one big block,

98
00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:57.340
because otherwise, individual data access is difficult

99
00:05:57.340 --> 00:06:01.500
and updates individual data items is usually not possible.

100
00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:05.520
So therefore, this rules out some different implementations.

101
00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:09.240
Okay, the third big part is reliability.

102
00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:13.400
So once we have written something into the database,

103
00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:16.740
and it's committed, we must never lose this again.

104
00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:20.100
So the database is supposed to be a very, very durable

105
00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:21.740
and reliable storage medium.

106
00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:24.400
So if you write, you will never lose this.

107
00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:30.100
even if we have a power failure, hardware crash, software failure.

108
00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:35.940
So even error situations we must survive and we must never lose data.

109
00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:41.820
So these are the requirements. And of course we have some more, but these are the three most important requirements.

110
00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:46.900
And now we must think about how do we get all this.

111
00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:57.640
Now what we could do is, we could just write one big program and say, okay this is my database and

112
00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:03.180
then just every time we do something when we go to disk, we read something from disk or we write

113
00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:07.360
something to disk, then we think about all these problems that we might have and then we hardcode

114
00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:15.500
them in this moment. This is not desirable. You should not do this. So implementing everything

115
00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:21.560
here in the slides because it's implementing it in bare metal, so directly on the device,

116
00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:30.840
is very difficult and also is not desirable because we want to be able to maintain and evolve the

117
00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:41.500
system. Like you see hardware changes over time. In the past, everybody had a hard disk and then

118
00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:43.440
In the case, same kind of case, some component,

119
00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:46.460
and then perhaps a cache, and then cache.

120
00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:49.140
I can't read this.

121
00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:51.780
Just write a C for cache.

122
00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:54.920
Then you have a cache, and then some other parts up top.

123
00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:57.680
So this is how traditionally the system was.

124
00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:00.560
Then over time, hardware changed.

125
00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:02.840
For nowadays, for example, and very frequently,

126
00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:04.480
you don't have a hard disk anymore.

127
00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:05.520
You have an SSD.

128
00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:10.700
And you could say SSD, hard disk, all the same.

129
00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:15.080
It's not completely true. These are very similar, but they're not the same.

130
00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:17.960
SSD has different performance characteristics,

131
00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:21.720
and you might want to change something in this system if you have an SSD.

132
00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:26.320
But nevertheless, you want to be able to replace one hardware technology with the other

133
00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:28.600
without rewriting the rest of the stack.

134
00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:33.380
Because on top of this, we might have something here like query processing and whatever.

135
00:08:33.980 --> 00:08:38.840
And we don't want to rewrite everything just because I switched from hard disks to SSD.

136
00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:41.260
So we must decouple them somehow.

137
00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:48.560
And the traditional way to do this, and actually it's a pretty good idea to do this, is to

138
00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:50.200
use a so-called layer architecture.

139
00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:58.040
So which means you cut your database into different layers of functionality, and each

140
00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:06.420
layer implements some specific feature, and the layers only talk to each other, only talk

141
00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:08.160
to layers that are directly next to each other.

142
00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:14.040
So you have here layer 1, so you have layer 2, and you have layer 3, and so on.

143
00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:20.020
And we say that these two talk to each other, and these two talk to each other,

144
00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:23.060
but layer 3 does not talk to layer 1.

145
00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:28.480
And if you build such an architecture, you can replace layers relatively easily.

146
00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:34.920
Because if I, for example, replace my layer L1 with a new implementation, let's call it L1',

147
00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:40.620
we for example decided that hard disks are old-fashioned, we no longer want to do this,

148
00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:46.920
we do everything in SSDs and because we know that for example that SSDs only support block

149
00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:53.920
arrays then so we cannot erase an arbitrary small piece of data but just blocks, therefore we change

150
00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:59.500
some implementation things. We might want to do this and if we do this we have to change something

151
00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:04.380
in L2 because L2 now must be aware of this property, but L3 is unaffected.

152
00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:10.440
This means that changes don't evolve through the whole code base, but just to neighboring layers.

153
00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:15.380
This is a very good property. So a layer architecture is very frequently a good idea.

154
00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:22.320
So if you can do this, writing systems in layers is a good thing. Now you might know a similar layer

155
00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:26.740
architecture, for example, from the network stack, which also builds pieces upon pieces.

156
00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:31.560
Like on the lowest layer you might have something like IPs and on top of this you might have

157
00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:33.240
UDP or TCP or this kind of thing.

158
00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:38.060
On top of this you have applications so you stack them on top of each other and you do

159
00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:39.560
it similar for a database system.

160
00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:40.360
Okay?

161
00:10:42.020 --> 00:10:44.300
Now the question is which layers do we have?

162
00:10:46.780 --> 00:10:52.300
This is not always the same with different systems cut it somewhat differently but as

163
00:10:52.300 --> 00:10:56.460
you will see on the slide because it's just an overview but on the next slide

164
00:10:57.380 --> 00:11:01.200
many systems tend to have a similar layer architecture not exactly the same

165
00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:06.020
but very similar but first let's look at a very simplified layer architecture

166
00:11:06.020 --> 00:11:10.800
this just gives you an idea what we have. Then we say on the lowest level here of

167
00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:16.300
course we have to see this is this cylinder here this is my physical

168
00:11:16.300 --> 00:11:19.660
storage at some point of course I have a right data to some physical medium

169
00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:28.080
SSD or hard disk. Now on top of this we have the storage layer. So this is what we are

170
00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:32.500
currently looking at in the lecture. So storage layer is what we are interested in. And as a

171
00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:40.980
component there is we have a database buffer which means the database buffers data in memory instead

172
00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:46.180
of going to disk because of course memory is much faster than disk. So we buffer in memory and we

173
00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:51.680
talk to the hardware below us, we orchestrate the read and write from

174
00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:58.420
physical storage. So this is the functionality of the storage layer and the storage layer

175
00:11:58.420 --> 00:12:04.740
exposes to the next layer pages. As you hopefully remember from your introduction

176
00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:10.220
to databases, that databases tend to store data organized in pages. We of

177
00:12:10.220 --> 00:12:14.680
course we also repeat this in the lecture, but so we have a fixed sized page that

178
00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:23.080
here and using this page as the next layer, so called access layer, now manages

179
00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:29.300
records which means individual tuples and access pairs, so index structures and

180
00:12:29.300 --> 00:12:34.200
extents and these kind of things. This is what the access layer does and the

181
00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:39.360
access layer exposes to the next higher layer records. The access layer

182
00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:43.900
says, okay we have a set of records you can read this or you can write this and

183
00:12:43.900 --> 00:13:00.280
And sitting on top of this we have the query layer. So the query layer now this runs queries, so performance translates SQL into some executable form, performance optimization and so on. And this runs this query. And this just thinks in records.

184
00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:06.200
And then of course, to expose again one step piers to the application.

185
00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:10.660
The interface of the application is now declarative queries and set of records.

186
00:13:10.900 --> 00:13:14.320
So we get declarative queries in and we get sets of records out.

187
00:13:15.620 --> 00:13:18.920
So this is a very simple layer architecture.

188
00:13:19.180 --> 00:13:22.580
We say storage is everything that talks about pages.

189
00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:25.940
Access layer does everything that talks about records.

190
00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:29.820
And query layer talks about queries and sets of records as results.

191
00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:36.000
a bit simplified but this makes it easy to understand. And now if you look at this

192
00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:40.740
layer architecture closer you see that the query layer knows nothing, at least

193
00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:44.640
in the first approximation, knows nothing about the storage layer. So if we should

194
00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:50.500
decide to say, oh those pages, we don't want to do pages, instead let's do, what

195
00:13:50.500 --> 00:13:54.960
could we actually do? I mean everybody basically does pages but we could decide

196
00:13:55.960 --> 00:14:13.260
say we use instead we just map records in memory. So if you decide to do this we

197
00:14:13.260 --> 00:14:20.600
don't do a paging anymore we just directly map physical record in my

198
00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:26.360
memory for example using a map or something similar techniques so if you

199
00:14:26.360 --> 00:14:30.140
want to do this I'm not saying it's a good idea to do this here but you could

200
00:14:30.140 --> 00:14:34.280
you could implement it like this and then we would have to change the excess

201
00:14:34.280 --> 00:14:37.600
layer because of course excess layer would be affected but the query layer

202
00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:41.760
knows nothing about this so clearly it doesn't care yeah so this means we can

203
00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:45.900
do even such a drastic change and this is a drastic change we can do such a

204
00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:50.080
drastic change without rewriting the whole system we just have to adjust some

205
00:14:50.780 --> 00:14:55.780
This is the benefits of using a layer architecture.

206
00:14:58.160 --> 00:15:03.620
Now what we have seen is that layers can be characterized by the data items that they manipulate.

207
00:15:03.960 --> 00:15:07.180
We said the lowest layer talks about pages, next one about records and so on.

208
00:15:08.460 --> 00:15:11.600
A lower layer offers functionalities to the next higher layer.

209
00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:17.840
They build more and more high-level constructs based upon the lower layers.

210
00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:22.280
and this means that each layer has only limited complexity.

211
00:15:22.480 --> 00:15:25.400
And this is good, because we want that layers are something

212
00:15:25.400 --> 00:15:26.960
that we can think about what they're doing,

213
00:15:27.080 --> 00:15:28.920
we understand them, we can change them.

214
00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:30.340
Changing is a pretty important thing.

215
00:15:30.700 --> 00:15:32.520
Database systems have to evolve over time

216
00:15:32.520 --> 00:15:36.380
because of use cases change, hardware change, and so on.

217
00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:37.780
We have to evolve the system,

218
00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:40.200
and therefore we want to keep the complexity manageable.

219
00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:44.340
Yeah, it's very roughly what we've seen is

220
00:15:44.340 --> 00:15:45.900
we start with a physical layer,

221
00:15:47.820 --> 00:15:57.720
Now, some talking to the rating system, then on top of this we have a low level functionality, like records, then high level we have queries.

222
00:15:58.460 --> 00:15:58.520
Okay?

223
00:15:59.380 --> 00:16:06.280
Now, this architecture in general makes sense, so you want to build it roughly like this, but the architecture is too simple.

224
00:16:06.640 --> 00:16:08.200
In reality, you need more components.

225
00:16:09.680 --> 00:16:13.420
Therefore, we now look at a bit more detailed architecture.

226
00:16:13.420 --> 00:16:19.320
is still somewhat simplified, but it's a bit more detailed architecture that really looks

227
00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:26.920
like existing systems, roughly. Okay, so this is a more detailed architecture. Again, we start from

228
00:16:26.920 --> 00:16:36.420
the bottom, so we start with a physical storage, external storage, and now on top of this, we have

229
00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:41.460
the file interface.

230
00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:45.160
And we say the access from the outside,

231
00:16:45.360 --> 00:16:48.600
so what comes in is, somebody says I want to read block K

232
00:16:48.600 --> 00:16:50.280
and I want to write block K.

